module gunbc.compute.remote_build_hosts

import std.types { Bool, List }
import product.host_identity { HostIdentity }
import gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_identity { operator_host_srv1, operator_host_srv2, operator_host_srv3, operator_host_srv4, operator_host_e2d4b7 }

// THE HOSTS THAT SERVE REMOTE BUILDS, AS THE ONE ROW BOTH SIDES OF THE SHARE READ. A serving host runs
// the remote-build account (gunbc.fleet.fleet_posix_accounts fleet_posix_remote_build_user) beside the
// operator's service user, and both reserve memory in the host's ONE capacity ledger. Two facts follow from
// this module's rows, and each is read at its own row rather than restated:
//   - gunbc.compute.host_capacity host_capacity_home_for_host resolves that host's ledger to the host-scoped
//     home instead of walking a dashboard instance, and gunbc.compute.remote_build puts the host's serving
//     instance on the roster -- both read THIS list, the live dispatch gate; AND
//   - gunbc.compute.host_capacity groups that host's ledger directories to the ledger group so the
//     remote-build account can write them (compute_ledger_sharing) -- since 2026-11-20 that one reads the
//     CANDIDATE row below, not this one. Keying a POSIX posture on dispatch eligibility was a fork: a host
//     taken off the dispatch gate while its serving account, its capacity home and its ledger content are
//     still on the disk needs that ledger to stay group-reachable, and the serving setup's own area rows
//     already use the shared posture for a candidate. The account witness
//     the_shared_ledger_grants_exactly_its_two_areas is the claim that caught it, when srv1 left this list.
// It sits below both modules because host_capacity cannot import the roster (the roster reaches the
// provider, which reaches host_capacity).
//
// ADMISSION IS FAIL-CLOSED: A HOST IS ON THIS LIST ONLY WHEN ITS LEDGER HOME ALREADY EXISTS ON DISK,
// created and read back. gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed ("every path succeeds fully or fails with a
// typed, located diagnostic") and gunbc.design_argument::replacement_migration ("atomic describes the
// authority transition"): if a host were listed before its /opt/gunbc/compute/capacity existed, dispatch
// would resolve a ledger in a directory nothing made, and -- worse -- the host's OWN dashboard instances
// would start resolving their ledger to /opt/gunbc via host_capacity_home_for_host and the host's live
// compute provider would refuse, because the host home is not there yet. So the row and the directory
// are landed in ONE change: the converger that CREATES and READS BACK the home is the thing that adds
// the row. That converger is remote-build PR 5 (a separate child, gunbc.compute-serve-setup); it adopts
// srv1's existing home and creates the others.
//
// WHO IS LISTED, AND ON WHAT EVIDENCE. srv1 -- its home already existed (its dashboard instance lives at
// /opt/gunbc, so it does not migrate). srv3 and srv4 -- admitted once the serving converger
// (gunbc.compute.remote_build_serving_converge) had created their account, ledger home, store, checkout,
// rustup, toolchain and serve binary and re-observed each host settled. Listing them carves one CI runner
// slot on each into its build pool (gunbc.runner.runner_slot_desired host_build_pool_reservation): they
// carry no dashboard instance to hold their own reservation. srv2 is NOT listed: its runner is down and its
// serving setup is unobserved. Its ledger switch from /home/briansrls to the host home is the converger's
// actuation -- no in-flight reservation carries across the switch, so it should happen when srv2 has no
// builds in flight.
//
// FOLLOW-UP, NOT INVENTED HERE. butch (srv14) and bobo (srv15) have the operator's interest but not yet a
// place on this list, and the new Supermicro is being modeled in another lane (still-fox-404):
//   - bobo (srv15): the next step is its OS, not its key.
//     #gunbc.fleet.fleet_intent_network::host_lan_address answers nothing for srv15 and it has no row in
//     #gunbc.fleet.fleet_enrollment::fleet_enrollments, so it is outside
//     #gunbc.fleet.fleet_enrollment::runner_executor_hosts -- no address to dial, no host a run could be
//     pinned to. A scanned row joins
//     #gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_key_enrollment::fleet_host_key_enrollments once an installed bobo has an
//     address and a live runner, and not before.
//  - butch (srv14): the 192 GiB serving reservation that held it off this list was DISCHARGED on 2026-11-24 --
//    both retirement triggers landed and the operator ruled the release (gunbc.design_ruling::
//    srv14_serving_memory_charge_retirement_operator_approved_2026_11_24; dissolved_frontier marker in
//    gunbc.serving.serving_reservation). Its pool figure asks for no authoring, because
//    #gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation::gunbc_runner_committed_width derives it from butch's own read host
//    facts; the candidate row (#gunbc.compute.remote_build_hosts::remote_build_serving_candidate_hosts), observe,
//    converge, and this row -- the order srv3 and srv4 took -- are the remaining steps in the butch lane.
//   - the Supermicro joins when it has both an identity with an anchored row and a declared pool.
// Each joins by adding a row here the day it has an on-disk ledger home, an anchored row and a real pool
// figure -- never before.
//
// LEAVING, 2026-11-20, AND WHY A ROW IS NOT A PERMANENT FACT. A row here promises that the host SERVES WHAT
// THE ROW PROMISES, and the promise is only good as the read-back that admitted it (the integration concern
// below: admission follows an on-disk home, proven by re-observation). srv1's last such read-back is its
// 08:57Z serving converge, and the promise has moved since: its pinned checkout predates 2387c5493bb9, so it
// judges a `gate <label>` request with the ENTRY route's grammar and returns that route's refusal (the class
// is filed in the failure-mode shelf as a_stale_serving_host_re_reads_a_verb_it_does_not_know_as_another_operations_operand,
// PR #16797 -- named in prose, not with a @ref marker, because a marker to a declaration that is not on main
// yet fails the marked-reference step of the required gate),
// and its build pool holds a seat whose holder was killed before recording an exit, so it admits no work at
// all. Listing it makes the client's first candidate a host that cannot serve the request -- and a typed
// request-refusal is terminal in remote_build_next_step, so the request never reaches a host that could.
// Removal states what is true and costs no capacity: srv1 carries its own ledger, so its build pool was never
// carved (host_build_pool_is_carved stays false with the row or without it) and its slice ceiling is the
// standing user-work pool either way. Its ledger keeps its group-shared posture: the posture belongs to the
// CANDIDATE row below, not to this one, because the serving account, the capacity home and an unsettled seat are
// still on that disk (host_capacity compute_ledger_sharing_carrier, and the account witness
// a_suspended_host_is_still_a_provisioned_candidate).
// RETURN, NAMED SO IT IS NOT RE-ADMITTED ON A GUESS: a serving converge on srv1 at or after 2387c5493bb9 --
// the same run that executes the seat-recovery leg -- and the row comes back in the change that converge's
// read-back proves. Nothing here re-adds it on hope.
fn remote_build_serving_hosts() -> List<HostIdentity> {
  [
    "srv3" as HostIdentity,
    "srv4" as HostIdentity,
  ]
}
// THE CANDIDATE ROW: WHICH HOSTS THE SERVING SETUP IS PROVISIONED ON. Deliberately NOT the live dispatch gate
// above (-> remote_build_host_rows -> the fabric roster), which admits a host only after its serving setup has
// converged: admitting a host before its /opt/gunbc/compute/capacity home, store and account exist is fail-open
// (review 95791). The serving setup's OBSERVE arm must still read a host that is not yet rostered, so it takes
// its candidate set from THIS row: the hosts it will, in the apply arm, converge and then admit to the live
// roster one-by-one (each only after its home/store/account are created and read back).
//
// IT MOVED HERE FROM gunbc.compute.remote_build_serving_setup ON 2026-11-20 so the two rows of one distinction
// are read in one module, and because host_capacity cannot import the setup module (the setup reaches
// host_capacity) yet must answer the ledger-posture question from one row rather than from a copy of it.
//
// THE LEDGER POSTURE IS KEYED HERE, NOT ON THE DISPATCH GATE: gunbc.compute.host_capacity
// compute_ledger_sharing_carrier groups a candidate's host-home ledger for the remote-build account, because the
// account, the home and the ledger's own content outlive a dispatch suspension. srv1 is that host today: off the
// dispatch gate, still provisioned, and its ledger still holds a seat whose holder never recorded an exit. A host
// leaves THIS row only when the serving route is withdrawn from it -- account removed, home dealt with, ledger
// content settled.
fn remote_build_serving_candidate_hosts() -> List<HostIdentity> {
  // ken (operator_host_e2d4b7) joins the CANDIDATE set only: it is admitted for the OBSERVE/CONVERGE arms so
  // the converge can create its /opt/gunbc/compute/capacity home. This does NOT put ken on
  // remote_build_serving_hosts nor into client dispatch -- that roster stays the ONE live gate, and ken's
  // roster join is separately gated on its capacity ledger having a traversable home (the build-lane concern
  // at gunbc.integration_concern, decider the build lane). Its build seat is priced against MEASURED bytes --
  // gunbc.host / test.claim.fleet.ken_build_host_gate_witness proves two seats fit over the live 56 GiB
  // serving charge -- so the srv14 "no quiescent read" bar does not apply here.
  //
  // READ WITH THE POSTURE NOTE ABOVE: ken is a candidate rooted at the host capacity home, so its ledger is
  // planned group-shared for the remote-build account from this row -- which is what the converge needs to
  // write the home it is being admitted to create. Nothing acts on that yet: ken is x86 and has no converge
  // path, so the posture is a plan awaiting an executor, not a change to a live host.
  //
  // butch (operator_host_srv14) joins the CANDIDATE set only, on the same footing as ken: admitted for the
  // OBSERVE/CONVERGE arms so a peer executor (srv1) can create its /opt/gunbc/compute/capacity serving home.
  // This does NOT put butch on remote_build_serving_hosts nor into client dispatch -- that roster stays the
  // ONE live gate (its join is step 6, separately gated on the capacity ledger having a traversable home).
  // unlike ken (x86, no converge path, a plan awaiting an executor), butch IS arm64 with a real converge path
  // run by the peer, and its Strata serving engines were retired BY THE OPERATOR'S HAND -- recorded as
  // ServingUnitBootState UnitDisabled in gunbc.serving.serving_reservation::srv14_serving_unit_boot_states --
  // so its reserved serving charge is released and its build pool is what that released charge frees
  // (gunbc.compute.remote_build). Grounded in host reads, not the ruling row alone: run 45173386343
  // (fleet-converge remote_build_serving_observe host=srv1 subject_host=srv14) read butch over the tailnet --
  // known_hosts anchor materialized + content-verified, 'enrolled host=srv14
  // endpoint=butch.tailecbe08.ts.net provenance=scanned-first-contact' -- proving it joined and is reachable;
  // the boot_states rows are the retirement read.
  [operator_host_srv1, operator_host_srv2, operator_host_srv3, operator_host_srv4, operator_host_e2d4b7, operator_host_srv14]
}

// IS THE SERVING ROUTE PROVISIONED ON THIS HOST, whatever its dispatch standing? The question the ledger
// posture asks (host_capacity compute_ledger_sharing_carrier) and the question the serving setup's observe
// arm asks are the same question, so both ask it here rather than re-deriving membership.
fn remote_build_host_is_serving_candidate(host: HostIdentity) -> Bool {
  any(remote_build_serving_candidate_hosts(), h => h == host)
}

fn remote_build_host_is_serving(host: HostIdentity) -> Bool {
  remote_build_serving_hosts() |> any(h => h as String == host as String)
}
